#import "style.typ": *
#chapter[How divisibility counts prime powers]

== A local calculation <c13s01>

Over a discrete valuation ring with uniformizer $pi_0$, an inclusion of lattices of the same rank has elementary divisors $pi_0^(b_1),dots,pi_0^(b_r)$. The length of the quotient is $sum_i b_i$. For a square matrix of full rank this is the valuation of its determinant. Elementary divisors record divisibility of a family of columns even after integral changes of basis. They are thus adapted to the rational isometries and integral comparisons in Chapter 12.

Factorials are one way for large elementary divisors to arise. Differentiating a power series with integral coefficients $a$ times and evaluating at zero produces $a!$ times an integral coefficient. Counting multiples of $p,p^2,dots$ gives
$ v_(p)(a!)=sum_(i>=1) floor(frac(a,p^i)). $
For $a=20$ and $p=3$ this gives $6+2=8$. If a local symmetry retains only odd iterates, the corresponding odd-level count begins with $6$ and the $p^3$ term is zero. The discarded levels and the weight constraints in a theta kernel differ from those of a single factorial. The example explains the appearance of prime *powers* and a sum of positive parts in the actual estimate.

In the local calculation the theta kernel is expanded as a power series with polynomial coefficients. Highest-weight projections and differentiation impose multidegrees. After the integral substitutions, every coefficient which can contribute belongs to the additive group generated by terms with explicit factorial factors. Three sorts of factors occur, from the prescribed derivative orders, from repeated applications at a Frobenius level, and from the remaining orders distributed among the columns. Their indices satisfy capacity inequalities fixed by the highest weight. One bounds the valuation of every generating term, and uses the ultrametric triangle inequality to pass to the sum. This argument survives cancellation of coefficients.

The logarithmic error in a factorial bound has a simple origin. If $s_(p)(q)$ denotes the sum of the base-$p$ digits of $q$, then $v_(p)(q!)=frac(q-s_(p)(q),p-1)$ and $s_(p)(q)<=(p-1)(1+log_(p)(q+1))$. In the multivariate calculation the errors are summed over occupied slots. Upper bounds on occupied slots, along with the separation of Frobenius iterates from Chapter 11, allow these digit and rounding errors to be charged separately from the leading gains. This is where a bound for triples of visits pays for itself.

== A multivariable derivative and its valuation <c13s02>

Fix a local field $K$ with valuation $v$ normalized by $v(p)=1$, and write $O$ for its valuation ring. If the extension is ramified the image of $K^times$ can contain fractions in this normalization. A bound on $v$ is then a bound on divisibility by the corresponding power of a local uniformizer after conversion by the ramification index. Write $v(0)=+infinity$ when comparing coefficients. For a polynomial with coefficients in $K$, having Gauss valuation at least $b$ means that every coefficient has valuation at least $b$. This definition makes sense even when $b$ is not in the value group.

Let $F(X)=sum_alpha c_alpha X^alpha$ be a power series in finitely many variables. With multi-index $alpha=(alpha_1,dots,alpha_d)$ one has
$ (partial^alpha F)(0)=(product_i alpha_i!) c_alpha. $
Every monomial of exponent different from $alpha$ either disappears on differentiating or still has a positive power of a variable on evaluation at zero. This is also valid for formal series because only one coefficient survives. If the entries of a differential polynomial $P$ have integral coefficients in extra variables, application of $P(partial)$ followed by evaluation yields a *finite sum* of such terms. With normalized differential coordinates involving $v_0^(-1)$ per derivative, the contribution gains the additional factor $v_0^(-|alpha|)$. The pinned operator uses exactly this normalization. Thus a factorial gain can be partly spent before the final coefficient is measured.

For a numerical illustration, take $p=3$ and apply $partial_X^6 partial_Y^4$ to $X^6Y^4$ at zero. The value is $6!4!$ and its valuation is three: the first factorial contains two factors of three and the second contains one. If $v(v_0)=1/4$, the normalization would subtract $10/4$, leaving $1/2$. Such a number can occur over an extension of ramification index four. Relative to its uniformizer, the remaining integral exponent is two. No floor should be taken independently at each derivative. Rounding ten copies of $1/4$ before summing would give a different answer.

The calculation explains part of the structure of the formal valuation theorem. There are finite position sets grouped into orbits, polynomial column indices, and a nonnegative profile of derivative orders. At a given orbit position $s$ the distinguished derivative orders $e_(s,b)$ sum to a prescribed integer $A_s$. A Frobenius-level occupation $h_(s,i)$ is present only at selected source positions and at positive odd levels. Let the target of this occupation on a cyclic orbit be $s+i$. Two constraints in the release are, in simplified notation,
$ p^i h_(s,i) <= A_s, quad h_(s,i) <= C_(s+i). $
The first charges the source derivative capacity and the second charges the target capacity. Additional counts for pairs of selected positions share their source capacities with some of the $h_(s,i)$. Another count consumes at least $p$ units per occurrence from a residual low-weight budget. Finally, if the total multiplicity arriving at $s$ is $H_s$, the residual column orders $b_(s,a,b)$ are nonnegative with sum $L_s-H_s$. These conditions couple the choices. Maximizing each local occupation separately can violate a pair constraint or make a residual sum negative. #src("Formalism.PeriodIntegrals.UnitaryGroupMassFormula.CongruenceGainBound.Development")[The full count constraints.]

Once these conditions hold, each generating coefficient in the theorem has the form
$ v_0^(-D) p^(-M)
   product_(s,b) e_(s,b)! dot product_(s,i) h_(s,i)!
       dot product_(s,a,b) b_(s,a,b)! $
times a polynomial with integral coefficients. Here $D$ is the total differential degree, and $M$ counts the repeated Frobenius and other denominator contributions. Orbits have been suppressed from the index list. In the source the formula specifies all indices, and the theorem asserts membership in the additive subgroup generated by such elements. This is a stronger structural statement than saying an entry may be estimated by one particular monomial. A general entry can be a signed sum of many generators with different profiles.

Let $A_b$ be the set of polynomials of Gauss valuation at least $b$. It contains zero, is closed under additive inversion, and is closed under addition by the nonarchimedean triangle inequality applied to each coefficient. If every generator lies in $A_b$, its additive subgroup lies there. The formal proof has three steps with just these meanings. It reduces a differentiated theta series to a linear combination of admissible model terms, expresses such terms in the subgroup generated by the count profiles, and estimates the valuations of all its generators. The first two steps use the structure of the integral kernel and the weight projections. Only the third uses the factorial inequalities. In particular a cancellation between generators may improve the estimate and cannot worsen it. #src("Formalism.PeriodIntegrals.UnitaryGroupMassFormula.CongruenceGainBound.HitsOf")[Generators with their three factorial products.] #src("Formalism.PeriodIntegrals.UnitaryGroupMassFormula.CongruenceGainBound.Development")[The assembly of the valuation bound.]

== Turning digit sums into an error allowance <c13s03>

For a nonnegative integer $a$ write $a=sum_j a_j p^j$ in base $p$, with $0<=a_j<p$. Counting positive multiples of each $p^j$ in a factorial gives
$ v_(p)(a!)=sum_(j>=1) floor(frac(a,p^j))
             =frac(a-s_(p)(a),p-1), quad s_(p)(a)=sum_j a_j. $
For the second equality, substitute the digit expansion into each floor. A digit $a_h$ with $h>=1$ contributes $a_(h)(1+p+dots+p^(h-1))=frac(a_h (p^h-1),p-1)$. Adding over $h$ gives the formula. For $a>=1$ there are at most $1+floor(log_(p)(a))$ digits. Therefore replacing the valuation by $frac(a,p-1)$ costs at most $1+log_(p)(a)$, while the cost at $a=0$ is zero.

For a product of many factorials $a_1!dots a_R!$, with each $a_i<=A$, add these bounds to obtain
$ v_(p)(product_i a_i!) >= frac(sum_i a_i,p-1)
      - |{i:a_i>0}|(1+log_(p)(max(1,A))). $
In particular replacing a sum over occupied slots by a sum over *all* slots can be expensive. There may be $f$ embeddings, order $N_m^2$ matrix coordinates, and a second index recording Frobenius levels. As an illustration, make $A$ single-unit occupations in different slots. All $a_i!$ are one and the valuation is zero. Putting all $A$ units into a single slot instead gives $v_(p)(A!)$ close to $frac(A,p-1)$. Total mass alone cannot decide between these extremes. A proof based on this inequality needs bounds on the number of occupied slots as well as on their total mass.

An additional error is caused by passing from $floor(frac(a,p^i))$ to $frac(a,p^i)$. A fixed $a$ has at most $floor(log_(p)(a))$ levels with $p^i<=a$, so the sum of these rounding errors is at most that many. In the released local comparison one allowance is
$ f_p=C f N_m^2 log_(p)(a)+C f (log_(p)(a))^2. $
The first term has the size of an entry count times a level depth. The second allows two depth indices. Both are local errors *before* summation over eligible primes. Keeping the base $p$ in these logarithms matters: after multiplication by $log p$ a factor $log_(p)(a)$ becomes $log a$. Replacing it at the outset by $log_(2)(a)$ and then adding the logarithmic prime weights would pay twice for the base change. #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.GlobalTheoremInputs")[The floor allowance.]

== An exact calculation at the third level <c13s04>

A formal series appearing in the local theory gives a more sensitive example of the difference between degree and valuation. Work over $ℚ$ with $p$ an odd prime and set
$ L(U)=sum_(i>=0) frac(U^((p^2)^i),p^i), quad L(T(X))=X, quad T(0)=0. $
Since $L(U)=U+$ terms of higher degree, there is a unique inverse for composition. Successive substitution determines its coefficients: after the coefficients of degrees below $n$ are known, the degree-$n$ term of $L(T)$ contains the unknown degree-$n$ coefficient of $T$ with coefficient one and a previously determined expression. In the auxiliary series
$ Z(X)=sum_(i>=0) frac(T(X)^(p(p^2)^i),p^(i+1)) $
one can compute the valuation of the coefficient of $X^(p^3)$ exactly. The answer is $-(p+1)$. This example from the released formal-series development probes a single normalization. The global gain theorem uses bounds for a system of such local calculations. #src("Formalism.BehrendSets.StatementE")[LevelThree.]

For completeness we derive the inverse-series coefficient identity needed here. For $N>=r>=1$, comparison of formal residues gives
$ [X^N] T(X)^r = frac(r,N) [U^(N-r)] (frac(U,L(U)))^N. $
Indeed the left side is the coefficient of $X^(-1)$ in $T(X)^r X^(-N-1)$. Under the change $X=L(U)$ the residue includes the factor $L'(U)$. Because a formal derivative has residue zero, differentiating $U^r L(U)^(-N)$ shows that the resulting residue equals $r/N$ times that of $U^(r-1)L(U)^(-N)$. Factoring out $U^(-N)$ gives the desired coefficient. All operations for a fixed degree involve only finitely many coefficients. The change of variable for residues itself follows by checking monomials: $L(U)^a L'(U)$ is a derivative divided by $a+1$ when $a!=-1$, and $frac(L'(U),L(U))$ has residue one.

For $N=p^3,r=p$ the required degree is $p(p^2-1)$. In this range $frac(L(U),U)$ may be replaced by $1+frac(U^(p^2-1),p)$ since the next term has degree $p^4-1$. The negative binomial expansion gives
$ [X^(p^3)] frac(T(X)^p,p)
    =frac((-1)^p,p^(p+3)) binom(p^3+p-1,p). $
The binomial coefficient has valuation two. Its factorial product can be written as $p^2 product_(j=1)^(p-1) frac(p^3+j,j)$, and all displayed ratios are $p$-adic units. Hence the valuation of this contribution is $2-(p+3)=-(p+1)$. The only other contribution at degree $p^3$ comes from $frac(T(X)^(p^3),p^2)$. Since $T$ has leading coefficient one, it is exactly $1/p^2$. Its valuation $-2$ is strictly larger for odd $p$. Two terms of distinct valuations have a sum of valuation equal to the smaller valuation. This proves the assertion. Counting the explicit denominators in $Z$ without tracing the inverse series would suggest only $-2$ and miss the larger denominator completely.

The same reasoning explains why the valuation proof treats normalized derivatives before replacing them by geometric sizes. A denominator can arise from using local coordinates adapted to the formal group even if the displayed group law was originally integral. The inverse series calculation is exact in a single degree. In a multivariable theta coefficient the number of possible degrees and substitutions grows with the ranks. The role of the count profiles is to bound the result uniformly when an explicit inverse calculation of each term would be impractical.

== The depth at which a residual prime power is discarded <c13s05>

The source distinguishes the error from counting factorial digits from the error in truncating very high powers at small inert primes. The latter uses the integer
$ b(p)=ceil(log_(p)(k^2 x^(3A)))+1 $
in the range $x,k>=1$, where $A$ is the fixed scale exponent and $p>=2$. In the formula for the local loss, a prime of inert class below the small-prime threshold contributes a multiple of $a p^(-b(p))$ inside one of the orbit errors. In the next range below $X'$ this part is replaced by $frac(a,p^2)$. The common other term in that same error is proportional to $frac(Λ_G a,p k)$, with an overall factor of $C log x$. These are components of the local comparison and are added to its other losses. The signal class has its own eligibility for the gain inequality; the two inert-only additions in the loss arise under their specified class tests. #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.GlobalTheoremInputs")[The least-power exponent and the piecewise local loss.]

Set $Y=k^2 x^(3A)>=1$. The defining rounding gives $log_(p)(Y)+1<=b(p)<log_(p)(Y)+2$. Since real exponentiation with base $p>1$ is increasing,
$ p Y <=p^(b(p))<p^2 Y, quad frac(1,p^2Y)<p^(-b(p))<=frac(1,p Y). $
This is a bound uniform in $p$, including primes for which $Y$ is very close to a power. If $Y$ itself is exactly $p^h$, the selected depth is $h+1$ and the first inequality is an equality. Omitting the final $+1$ would lose the full factor $1/p$ in the upper bound for the reciprocal. For example, with $p=3$ and $Y=81$, the two possible reciprocals are $1/243$ and $1/81$.

For a finite cutoff $P>=2$ it follows, using only the selection of the exponent, that
$ sum_(p<=P) (log p) a p^(-b(p))
     <=frac(a,Y)sum_(p<=P)frac(log p,p). $
Partial summation and the Chebyshev upper bound make the last prime sum at most a constant times $1+log P$. No assertion about equidistribution among Frobenius classes is needed for this positive upper bound. Restricting to any subclass of primes can only improve it. By contrast the contribution of the elementary high-prime tail is on the scale $a sum_(p>=P)frac(log p,p^2)$, bounded by a constant times $a/P$ by a dyadic decomposition and the Chebyshev estimate. The reason for using different formulas at the two ranges is visible in these two computations. Summing $p^(-2)$ at the smallest primes spends a fixed fraction of $a$. At an increasing lower cutoff it is cheap, while a deep prime power gives the required saving even when $p$ stays fixed.

== The arithmetic weight of an inert prime <c13s06>

Write $c=((1,1),0)$ for complex conjugation in $G=E_0 times H$. In these additive coordinates the first bit records the action on $sqrt(-d)$, so it is one at an unramified prime precisely when $chi_(d)(p)=-1$. A top place $t$ is represented by $t^+=((0,0),t)$. If the Frobenius coordinate $g_p$ of an unramified prime has nonzero first sign, then $i g_p$ lies outside $Phi$ precisely for odd $i$. Its partner $c+i g_p+t^+$ lies in $Phi$. For a lift $j$ the ideal prime sum associated with $t$ has the unramified contribution
$ 2 log p sum_(i>=1, i " odd") (frac(a_t,p^i)-w_(j)(c+i g_p+t^+))_+. $
The sums can be truncated whenever $frac(a_t,p^i)$ falls below the smallest weight. Primes that split in $ℚ(sqrt(-d))$ make no contribution to this unramified expression, independently of their behavior in the other coordinates. The ramified prime $d$ uses the inertia coset of $i g_d$ in place of the single $i g_d$, and each eligible element is weighted by $log d$. The construction deals separately with the distinguished primes at two and $q$. The definitions of the arithmetic frame specify their Frobenius and inertia terms. These local conventions are necessary for the Fourier identity in Chapter 17.

Why does the leading coefficient have a two? For the quadratic sign test, $1-chi_(d)(p)^i$ is two at an inert prime with odd $i$ and is zero at even $i$. At a ramified prime the character value is zero. In the class-by-class formula the inertia sum places the contribution at the right weights. For constant $w=1$ one can already recognize the factor $1-chi_(d)(n)$ in the ramp.

== How an odd level produces a positive part <c13s07>

The basic arithmetic alternation is visible already when all the local weight thresholds are one. An unramified inert prime has quadratic character $-1$. In a sum over positive powers $p^i$ the factor $1-(-1)^i$ selects the odd powers and doubles them. If the threshold at the relevant partner position is $W_i>=1$, the ideal contribution becomes
$ 2 log p sum_(i>=1, i " odd") (frac(a_t,p^i)-W_i)_+. $
For a fixed $i$ this is zero when $frac(a_t,p^i)<=W_i$ and increases linearly with $a_t$ above the threshold. The positive-part operation reflects a capacity available at one position after a corresponding capacity at the other has been consumed. Local integral estimates compare with this expression after subtracting the factorial, orbit, and lattice-replacement allowances. The weighted prime power lies at the *same* odd level as its Frobenius partner.

#example[Two visible levels at five][Take the arithmetic expression with $a_t=1000$, $p=5$, $W_1=7$, $W_3=2$, and $W_i>=1$ elsewhere. Its nonzero odd summands are $200-7=193$ and $8-2=6$. For $i>=5$, $frac(1000,5^i)<1$ and all vanish. The total, before other local costs, is $398 log 5$. If the two thresholds are interchanged the individual contributions change. Their sum becomes $198+1=199$, coincidentally the same here since neither positive part changes sign. In contrast, raising $W_3$ from two to ten kills its former contribution of six, an effect smaller than the increase of eight in its threshold. The breakpoint is exactly at eight.]

For every prime in the specified finite set $S_0$, which contains two and $q$, both the specified inertia subgroup and the chosen Frobenius belong to the index-two subgroup defining the CM type. This split condition is relative to the quadratic sign at $d$. A prime in $S_0$ can still ramify in the full host. Thus their full Frobenius cosets stay there and contribute no term in the complementary-sign sum. At the ramified prime $d$, inertia consists of two elements with opposite first sign. Each Frobenius coset contains one element of the complementary sign, even for an even power. The one such position contributes with coefficient $log d$. At an unramified inert prime the odd coset contains one complementary-sign element but its inertia order is one, so Fourier inversion gives $2 log p$. This comparison of inertia *orders* explains why substituting a quadratic character value zero at $d$ into an unramified partner formula is inadequate when weights depend on the partner. The partner itself has to be read from the inertia coset. #src("Formalism.SizeWindowBrackets.FirstPrimeNeSecondPrime")[The unramified and ramified sums.]

There is a useful elementary model for the capacity cost in a cycle even before performing the full valuation calculation. On a cyclic orbit of length $d$ with available capacities $D_s>=0$, introduce a nonnegative quantity $z_(s,i)$ at each odd positive level $i<d$. The feasibility inequalities of the release read
$ sum_(i<d) p^i z_(s,i)+sum_(i<d) z_(s-i,i) <= D_s. $
Only allowed indices enter. The first term uses capacity at the source and the second at the destination. Adding all inequalities over $s$ shows that one unit at level $i$ uses $p^i+1$ total units. The value $sum z_(s,i)$ is bounded, and taking its supremum makes sense. One of the arithmetic correction functions is $frac(p,p^2-1)sum_s D_s$ minus this supremum. In particular it is nonnegative because $frac(1,p+1)<=frac(p,p^2-1)$ and each allowable occupation consumes at least $p+1$ units. The zero occupation gives the elementary upper bound $frac(p,p^2-1)sum_s D_s$. #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.DataParams₂")[The feasible set and the correction function.]

For a length-two orbit only level one occurs. At $p=5$, take $D_0=10,D_1=7$. The inequalities become $5z_0+z_1<=10$ and $z_0+5z_1<=7$. Their sum bounds $z_0+z_1$ by $17/6$. Equality is achieved by $z_0=43/24,z_1=25/24$, both nonnegative. The correction is $85/24-17/6=17/24$. If instead $D_1=0$, both variables must vanish however large $D_0$ is. In that case the correction is $frac(5D_0,24)$. A model discarding the destination capacity would permit a positive flow even into an empty destination. It is precisely the placement of the large weights by Frobenius partners that affects this obstruction.

== A certificate for the maximum occupation <c13s08>

The supremum over occupations can be bounded without enumerating them. Consider an orbit with at least two positions, retain the previously allowed odd indices $0<i<d$, and let $lambda_s$ be a nonnegative real number at each position. Suppose the prices satisfy
$ p^i lambda_s+lambda_(s+i)>=1 $
for every allowed directed step $(s,i)$. Multiply the capacity inequality at position $s$ by $lambda_s$ and sum over all positions. The outgoing term charges $p^i lambda_s$ to one unit of $z_(s,i)$. After reindexing, the incoming term charges $lambda_(s+i)$ to the very same unit. Thus
$ sum_(s,i) z_(s,i) <= sum_(s,i)(p^i lambda_s+lambda_(s+i))z_(s,i)
               <=sum_s lambda_s D_s. $
Every choice of prices satisfying the displayed restrictions gives a valid upper bound. Every particular feasible occupation gives a lower bound. When two such bounds meet, the maximum and the cost correction are known exactly. This elementary certificate uses only finite sums of nonnegative terms, so it also applies when some capacities vanish or an optimum occurs on the boundary.

The universal choice $lambda_s=frac(1,p+1)$ works because $p^i>=p$. On an orbit with constant capacity $D$ the occupation $z_(s,1)=frac(D,p+1)$ at every position, with higher levels empty, uses exactly $D$ at each position. The upper and lower bounds coincide. The correction defined above is then
$ frac(p d D,p^2-1)-frac(d D,p+1)=frac(d D,p^2-1). $
An arbitrarily long cycle can realize the same per-position optimum. If $d=1$, however, there is no positive allowed index less than $d$; the supremum is zero and the constant-capacity expression for the correction changes. This is an example of a genuine short-orbit exception.

On the length-two orbit the entire range of the capacities can be resolved. Write $D_0=A$, $D_1=B$, with $A,B>=0$. In the middle range $A/p<=B<=p A$, saturating both constraints gives
$ z_0=frac(p A-B,p^2-1), quad z_1=frac(p B-A,p^2-1). $
Both are nonnegative, and their sum is $frac(A+B,p+1)$, the uniform-price upper bound. If $B<A/p$, the proposed value of $z_1$ is negative. Instead put $z_0=B$ and $z_1=0$. The source cost $p B$ is at most $A$ and the destination cost is exactly $B$. The prices $lambda_0=0$, $lambda_1=1$ are admissible and give the matching upper bound $B$. Interchanging the positions gives the answer $A$ when $B>p A$. Combining the ranges yields
$ sup(z_0+z_1)=min(A,B,frac(A+B,p+1)). $
The formula remains correct at $A=0$, $B=0$ and at both transition lines. The final minimum is continuous but changes slope at those lines. This is a finite-dimensional instance of the positive-part expressions arising when inequalities comparing powers of $p$ with partner weights are expanded.

For example at $p=5$, $A=50$, and $B=4$, the attainable value is four. The active directed step uses only twenty of the fifty units of source capacity and every unit of destination capacity. Increasing $A$ by one has no effect. Increasing $B$ by one increases the optimum by one until the boundary $B=10$. Beyond that boundary, as long as $B<=250$, a one-unit increase raises the optimum by only $1/6$. If a bound treated the fifty source units as freely usable, it would lose precisely the information carried by the location of its small partner. The actual derivative model contains additional integral constraints and costs. The calculation here solves the real capacity supremum in the correction term; it does not guarantee an individual coefficient attaining the valuation lower bound.

== What an entrywise divisor says about all minors <c13s09>

Suppose $R$ is a discrete valuation ring with uniformizer $ϖ$, and $B:R^a arrow.r R^b$ is an integral period map in lattice bases. If each entry is divisible by $ϖ^h$, division by this common power produces another integral map. A change of source or target basis by an invertible integral matrix preserves the property. Indeed every new entry is an integral linear combination of old entries, and applying the inverse basis change gives the converse. This basis independence is why divisibility can be built into a lattice comparison for the global family of periods.

More refined information is furnished by the determinantal ideals. For $1<=r<=min(a,b)$ let $I_(r)(B)$ be the ideal generated by its $r$-rowed square minors, with the zero ideal when they all vanish. Cauchy–Binet expresses each minor of $U B V$ as a sum of products of an $r$-minor of $U$, one of $B$, and one of $V$ over the intermediate row and column subsets. If $U,V$ are invertible over $R$ this first gives $I_(r)(U B V) subset.eq I_(r)(B)$. Applying the same argument to their inverses proves equality. The $r$th exterior power gives the same argument geometrically. Its matrix entries in wedge bases are exactly these minors. Dividing a map by $ϖ^h$ divides every entry of its $r$th exterior power by $ϖ^(r h)$.

Over a discrete valuation ring, elementary row and column operations give a diagonal matrix with nonzero diagonal entries $ϖ^(a_1),dots,ϖ^(a_t)$ ordered by increasing exponent. To obtain the first diagonal, choose an entry of least valuation. It divides all the other entries, so integral row and column operations clear its column and row. Repeating on the remaining block gives the diagonal after absorbing units. Every entry of the remaining block is still a multiple of the first pivot. By induction the exponents are nondecreasing. The least valuation among the nonzero $r$-minors is consequently $a_1+dots+a_r$ for $r<=t$. For $r=t$, this is the length of the quotient of the saturated image by the image. To see this directly in the diagonal basis, the saturated image consists of the first $t$ free coordinates, and the quotient is the direct sum of $R slash ϖ^(a_i) R$. A common entrywise exponent $h$ accounts for $t h$ units of that length. Extra divisibility or cancellation among the minors can increase it.

Take $R=ℤ_p$ and consider the square matrix
$ B=mat(p^2,p^2; p^2,p^2+p^3). $
Every entry has valuation at least two and one entry has valuation exactly two. Subtract the first row from the second and then the first column from the second. The resulting diagonal is $p^2,p^3$. The determinant valuation is five. An entrywise argument supplies four of these five units upon passing to rank two. The additional unit comes from the equality of the first two rows modulo $p^3$; it is seen by the determinant. Dividing the entire matrix by $p^2$ gives a new integral map with elementary exponents zero and one. In particular the divided map can still have a nontrivial integral cokernel even when one coordinate becomes a unit.

Now append a third row $(0,p^b)$ with an integer $b>=2$. The three maximal minors have valuations $5$, $2+b$, and $2+b$. The new image has rank two, and the least valuation is $min(5,2+b)$. At $b=2$ the two elementary exponents are two and two. At $b=4$ they are two and three. Appending an additional observation of a pair of periods has *reduced* the saturation length in the first case. There is no contradiction with the old determinant, which is still one of the maximal minors. It is the ideal of *all* the maximal minors that controls the rectangular map. This is relevant when period maps are stacked over test vectors or when a comparison uses an image generated by several words.

The corresponding archimedean operation has a different form. For a complex rectangular matrix $B$ with $a$ columns, $b>=a$, Cauchy–Binet gives $det(B^* B)=sum_I |det B_I|^2$, summing over row subsets of size $a$. The quantity on the right is a sum of nonnegative numbers. Over the discrete valuation ring the valuation of the *ideal* generated by the same minors is their minimum. Replacing that minimum by the valuation of a signed sum of minors can introduce cancellation. Conversely replacing the archimedean Gram determinant by the squared norm of a single favored minor gives only one side of a bound. An integral guaranteed divisor and a norm of a complex linear map therefore meet in the determinant method with different operations. Their precise normalization is needed when the slope estimate compares them.

== The net gain after all lifts <c13s10>

Each prime-power ramp is an ideal gain attached to one top place and one step. The geometric count comes from a finite place of a coefficient field, where only a subset of the top places may be served. Its lower bound must pay three costs with different multiplicities. It pays one allowance per served top and step, one floor allowance per step, and one replacement of the local lattice for the entire chain. The formal comparison applies at a signal prime or an inert-class prime of residue characteristic at most $X''$. At a signal prime, or when the characteristic reaches $X'$, the served set is the full top set.

To combine the places one weights a rational-prime valuation by the local degree and then sums over the finite support. Two exclusions enter this bookkeeping. A fixed drop set determines which gains are retained in the common total $G_a$. Separately, the comparison with the integral lattice belonging to a word omits its auxiliary prime $T_u$ from a local assertion. In fact the parameter record requires $T_u$ to lie outside the common drop set for every word. The formula below for $G_a$ accordingly has no word index. It is this one number that the next chapter compares with the period main term. #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.DataParams₂")[The drop set and the common gain.] #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.GlobalTheoremInputs")[The place, prime-class and word hypotheses.]

== Reading the net local inequality <c13s11>

For a fixed finite coefficient-field place $v$ of residue characteristic $p$, write $S_v$ for the top places served at $v$. The following comparison assumes that $p$ is in the signal class or the inert class and that $p<=X''$. For every step $j$ it bounds the gain in the interval
$ 0<=g_(j,v)<=4|T|frac(a,p). $
Things are subtler on the lower side. Put $A_(j,t,p)=sum_(i>=1, i " odd")(frac(a_t,p^i)-W_(j,t,p,i))_+$ with the prescribed local thresholds. If $tau_v$ is the replacement cost, the lower estimate is
$ sum_(j=1)^m sum_(t in S_v) (A_(j,t,p)-ℓ_p)-m f_p
      <=sum_(j=1)^m g_(j,v)-tau_v. $
The order of summation specifies who pays which error. The term $ℓ_p$ occurs for each served top and each step. The term $f_p$ occurs once per step at the current coefficient-field place. The replacement cost occurs once for the whole chain. Assigning an independent $tau_v$ to all $m$ steps would overcharge it by the factor needed for the gain. In the source $S_v$ equals the whole top set at a signal prime or when $p>=X'$, including the endpoint. Where fewer places are served, a separate orbit estimate bounds their missing contribution. #src("Formalism.SharpRestriction.GlobalTheoremInputs")[LinkG_at.] #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.DataParams₂")[Served top sets and the two cutoffs.]

The odd-power sum displayed above is finite in the parameter record. For integral $a_t>=1$ it uses a range of length $floor(log_(p)(a_t))+2$, counting from zero. The zeroth level fails the parity condition. At a level with $p^i>a_t$ a threshold of at least one makes the ramp zero. Including the extra endpoint is thus compatible with the infinite-sum notation whenever that lower bound on the threshold holds. #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.DataParams₂")[The finite von Mangoldt class sum.]

The prime-dependent error $ℓ_p$ has a common term proportional to $(S_c+k+h)log_(p)(2a)$, where $S_c$ is the spread scale for non-top weights and $h$ is the weight tolerance. The small-prime threshold in its inert-only corrections is $p_0=k^2 x^26$ and the cutoff used to discard residual prime powers is $Y=k^2 x^78$. There are two comparisons of sizes here. The orbit term containing $p^(-b(p))$ or $p^(-2)$ is present only for the inert class with $p<X'$. The other class-dependent term checks $p<p_0$ first, and then checks $p<X'$ in its remaining range. At a signal prime the common error still appears, so setting both inert-only additions to zero does not set the entire local error to zero. This bookkeeping matters when the local inequality is summed over all eligible prime classes.

Let $D=[K:ℚ]$, and write $e_v,f_v$ for the ramification index and residue degree over $p$. The normalization of the coefficient-field place used here is
$ lambda_v=frac(log op("N")(v),D)=frac(f_v log p,D), quad
       cal("S")_(v)(y)=2 lambda_v e_v y. $
Multiply a lower bound in rational-prime valuation units by $cal("S")_(v)(1)$ to put it into the logarithmic degree comparison. The factor $e_v$ converts rational-prime units to uniformizer units. The identity $sum_(v divides p)e_v f_v=D$ then gives
$ sum_(v divides p) cal("S")_(v)(y)=2 y log p $
for a real $y$ independent of $v$. If $y$ varies with $v$, the result is $2 log p$ times the average with weights $frac(e_v f_v,D)$. The arithmetic sum over embeddings and the sum over coefficient-field places therefore have distinct indices. Doing the latter first with a constant coefficient can simplify the normalization but does not turn a place-dependent bound into an unweighted average.

These weights pass a field-extension test. Let $K'/K$ be a finite extension of coefficient fields of degree $d$. Suppose that for each place $w$ of $K'$ above $v$ the numerical valuation in rational-prime units is the same $y_v$. Write $e_(w/v),f_(w/v)$ for the relative ramification index and residue degree. Then $e_w=e_v e_(w/v)$, $f_w=f_v f_(w/v)$, and $sum_(w divides v)e_(w/v)f_(w/v)=d$. The last identity follows by taking $K_v$-dimensions in the decomposition of $K' ⊗_K K_v$ as the product of the completions at $w$. Since $[K':ℚ]=d D$, one finds
$ sum_(w divides v) frac(2 e_w f_w log p,d D)y_v
       =frac(2e_v f_v log p,D)y_v. $
Every term could change, but their total over a given original place is invariant. For example a place that splits into $d$ places contributes $d$ summands each of weight $1/d$ times its original weight. At a totally ramified place there is a single new summand, and growth of the ramification index cancels growth of the field degree. Omitting the ramification index preserves the split example but fails on the totally ramified example. This calculation concerns extension of a common scalar or of a numerical valuation known to pull back in this way. If a lattice, a local correction or a set of served embeddings changes with the extended data, compatibility of those values is a further mathematical claim.

For example take $K=ℚ(i)$ and $p=5$. There are two split coefficient places, each of norm five, with $e_v=f_v=1$. A common valuation $y$ contributes $y log 5$ at each place, giving $2y log 5$. At $p=3$ there is one inert place of norm nine. Its weight is $frac(2 log 9,2)=2 log 3$ in rational-prime units. At $p=2$ the place has norm two and $e_v=2$, giving the same expression $2 log 2$. These are examples of the *coefficient field* normalization; calling a coefficient place “inert” here is independent of whether that rational prime belongs to the signal or inert class in the extension defining the theta data. Both fields can occur in the same formula with different purposes. #src("Formalism.LocalRingCardinality.DataArith")[Place weights and the normalization identity.]

To form the global retained gain, discard the indicated rational prime exclusions before summing over the steps. The precise quantity is
$ G_a=sum_(v in S) cal("S")_(v)(sum_(j=1)^m g'_(j,v)-tau_v), $
where $S$ is the finite support and $g'_(j,v)$ equals $g_(j,v)$ unless the residue characteristic lies in the fixed drop set, where it equals zero. The parameter record keeps every word prime $T_u$ outside that set. The separate exclusion $p!=T_u$ belongs to the lattice comparison for that word. To see the distinction in the accounting, expand the local word contribution as $sum_(j)(g'_(j,v)+F_(j,v)-E_(u,j,v))$. Summing its weighted values over $S$ and subtracting the weighted replacement costs gives exactly $G_a+F_o-E_u$. The weighted sum of the $F_(j,v)$ is $F_o$, common to the realization. The weighted sum of the $E_(u,j,v)$ is the cost $E_u$ for this word. Some places give zero in one term and nonzero in another. In particular the gains themselves vanish at the two auxiliary Hecke primes, while the distinguished-place correction remembers their logarithms. This separation lets the Hecke contribution be canceled with a word-count contribution measured at infinity.

There are two logically different reasons for a local estimate to become small after global summation. Some losses are individually tiny, for example a term with the explicit power $p^(-b(p))$. Other losses can be large for a particular orbit but occur on few top places or are infrequent across translations. The first are summed using numerical bounds; the second require the spacing and weighted selection of Chapter 11. Once the arithmetic comparison has bounded the retained gain from below in this manner, the slope calculation can bound the *same* quantity from above. The residual quantity after subtracting the period main term will then be recognized as the centered prime sum.

